INT. GREENHOUSE ABOVE A CLOSED TRAIN STATION - LATE AFTERNOON

The glass roof has been mended in panes of different greens. Tomato vines climb strings tied to old signal wires. Down below, a freight train sighs through where no passenger train has stopped in years. The floor trembles gently.

IMELDA CORREIA (70s, small, hands like roots) kneels in the dirt, tying a vine. Her daughter-in-law, NOOR HASSAN (40s, good coat, car keys held too long) stands at the doorway and does not step in.

IMELDA
Shoes.

Noor looks at her shoes, clean leather, and takes them off. Sets them side by side on the threshold. Walks in on stocking feet.

NOOR
It's warm in here.

IMELDA
It is a greenhouse.

Noor touches a leaf. Rubs it between finger and thumb. Smells it. Imelda watches her without turning her head.

NOOR
Teo says you haven't been answering.

IMELDA
The telephone is in the kitchen. I am not in the kitchen.

NOOR
He's worried.

IMELDA
He is busy.

Noor smiles at that, thin and brief. At the far end of the greenhouse, a boy of about twelve, TEODORO "TEO" CORREIA-HASSAN (12, serious, glasses taped at the hinge), sits cross-legged between two rows of lettuce, sorting seed packets by colour and not looking up.

NOOR
He came with me.

IMELDA
I saw him.

NOOR
He wouldn't come over.

IMELDA
He is where he is supposed to be.

A long silence. The freight train hushes the room, then passes. Noor lowers herself onto an overturned bucket.

NOOR
Marcos called.

Imelda ties a knot. Pulls it tight. Snips the string with her teeth.

IMELDA
How is the weather in Lisbon?

NOOR
He didn't say.

IMELDA
Then he did not call for long.

NOOR
He called for four minutes. He asked about the house.

IMELDA
The house is standing.

NOOR
He asked whether you were thinking of selling.

Imelda reaches for a tomato, turns it in her palm, and lets it go. She picks another. Presses it. Hard. Green. She leaves it.

IMELDA
It is a little early for these.

NOOR
Imelda.

IMELDA
Three more weeks. I will make the sauce for the church supper. Father Anselmo likes it with basil. I told him I would bring basil.

NOOR
You don't have to answer me.

IMELDA
No.

She gets up slowly, one hand on her knee. Crosses the greenhouse. Takes a clay pot from a shelf and carries it back to Noor, who takes it without being asked. A basil plant, small and fierce.

IMELDA (CONT'D)
Put that in your window. It does not like the dark.

Noor holds the pot in both hands like something warm.

NOOR
There isn't much window in the apartment.

IMELDA
Then it will grow toward what there is.

Across the glass, Teo has lined up all the packets in a spectrum. He pushes his glasses up with one knuckle and looks at the two women for the first time. Then back to his seeds.

The door rattles. FATHER ANSELMO (60s, round, cassock under a windbreaker) leans in with a bag of potting soil on his shoulder, and behind him GRETA VOLKOV (50s, neighbour, gardening gloves, loud scarf).

FATHER ANSELMO
Imelda, the basil?

IMELDA
On the shelf. Shoes.

GRETA
We already took them off. Hello, Noor.

Noor nods. Father Anselmo sets down the soil and goes straight to the shelf, humming.

NOOR
I should go. He has school tomorrow.

IMELDA
He has school tomorrow.

Neither moves.

IMELDA (CONT'D)
Wait.

She goes to the end of a row and snaps off a tomato vine with two small green fruits on it. Wraps the stem in a wet paper towel. Holds it out.

IMELDA (CONT'D)
For his windowsill. Not for eating.

Noor takes it. Their fingers touch. Neither pulls back at once.

NOOR
Thank you.

IMELDA
Take your shoes.

Noor goes to the threshold, steps into her shoes, and turns once to look at the rows of green. Then she crosses to Teo and crouches beside him. He holds out one packet, wordless. She reads the label, nods, tucks it in her coat.

Imelda has already turned back to the vines. She hums something under her breath, badly, and ties another knot.

The next freight train begins to build, far off, in the rails.
